          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Babel/Babylon symbolizes prideful, self-exalting human civilization organized against God, especially an idolatrous and oppressive empire or world system destined for judgment.\",\"book_summary\":\"Babel/Babylon is a self-made anti-heaven kingdom that seeks its own name, dominates other kingdoms, and counterfeits God’s covenant order.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The archetypal anti-God human kingdom or imperial civilization founded on pride and rebellion.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A self-created anti-heaven kingdom and empire that imitates or rivals God’s covenant kingdom.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement is evident. The phrase “covenant counterfeit” adds a theological characterization but does not clearly add, exclude, or reclassify cases covered by the consensus.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both identify Babel/Babylon with self-exalting human kingdom-building opposed to God and expressed through imperial power.\",\"rationale\":\"The book’s “builds a name against heaven” closely restates the consensus reading of Genesis 11, while “empire raised over many kingdoms” corresponds to the established Babylon-as-oppressive-world-empire symbolism. Calling it “the covenant counterfeit” sharpens the contrast between Babylon and God’s kingdom but remains compatible with the consensus’s city-of-man versus city-of-God framework.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts strongly support self-made fame, international imperial influence, and judgment, but they do not by themselves explicitly establish the more specific description “covenant counterfeit.”\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
